    "lem-rpm-low-speed-remainders": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-mean-value",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f, g : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as functions on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$\\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) \\;=\\; \\bigl(g(b)-g(a)\\bigr)\\,f'(c) .$$\n\n**The statement is a product identity, and that is deliberate.** The familiar\nquotient form\n\n$$\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{g(b)-g(a)} \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)}{g'(c)}$$\n\nis not asserted here, and it is not equivalent: its left side needs\n$g(b) \\ne g(a)$ and its right side needs $g'(c) \\ne 0$, and neither follows from\nthe hypotheses. The product form above needs neither, holds under exactly the\nhypotheses stated, and specialises to the quotient form whenever both\ndenominators happen to be nonzero. The companion page exhibits an $f$ and a $g$\nfor which the quotient form is meaningless while the product form holds.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
          "claim": "On $[0,\\delta^2]$ define $f(s)=(1-s)^{-1/2}$. Since $1-s>0$ throughout a neighborhood of that interval, [L1] and [L2] show that $f$ is continuous there, differentiable on $(0,\\delta^2)$, and $f'(s)=\\frac12(1-s)^{-3/2}$, $f''(s)=\\frac34(1-s)^{-5/2}$. Both derivatives are positive. Differentiating the displayed powers gives $f'''(s)=\\frac{15}{8}(1-s)^{-7/2}>0$ on $(0,\\delta^2)$, so $f''$ is increasing there; the formula for $f''$ also shows that $f'$ is increasing.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2.1",
          "claim": "If $z=0$, then $v=0$, $\\gamma=1$, $p=0$, and $E=mc^2$, so every asserted inequality holds with equality. If $z>0$, [L3] applied to $f$ on $[0,z]$ gives $\\xi\\in(0,z)$ with $\\gamma-1=f'(\\xi)z$. Since $f'(\\xi)>0$ and $f'(\\xi)\\le f'(\\delta^2)$ by the monotonicity established in step 1.1, this proves $0\\le\\gamma-1\\le z/[2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}]$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "L3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3.1",
          "claim": "For $z>0$, define $q(s)=f(s)-1-s/2$ and $g(s)=s^2$ on $[0,z]$. Cauchy's mean value theorem [L4] gives $\\xi\\in(0,z)$ such that $q(z)g'(\\xi)=g(z)q'(\\xi)$, because $q(0)=g(0)=0$. Since $g'(\\xi)=2\\xi>0$, this is $q(z)=z^2(f'(\\xi)-1/2)/(2\\xi)$. Applying [L3] to $f'$ on $[0,\\xi]$ gives $\\eta\\in(0,\\xi)$ with $f'(\\xi)-f'(0)=\\xi f''(\\eta)$. As $f'(0)=1/2$, substitution yields $q(z)=\\frac12 f''(\\eta)z^2$. Thus $q(z)\\ge0$ and, because $f''$ is increasing and $\\eta<z\\le\\delta^2$, $q(z)\\le\\frac12 f''(\\delta^2)z^2=3z^2/[8(1-\\delta^2)^{5/2}]$. For $z=0$ the same bound is equality by step 2.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "L3",
            "L4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4.1",
          "claim": "The definitions give $|p-mv|=m|v|(\\gamma-1)$ and $E-mc^2-\\frac12m|v|^2=mc^2(\\gamma-1-z/2)$. Applying steps 2.1 and 3.1, multiplying by the nonnegative factors $m|v|$ and $mc^2$, and substituting $z=|v|^2/c^2$ gives the two remaining inequalities. Their lower bounds follow from the nonnegativity in those same steps.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
