    "pthm-rpm-prescribed-em-nr-trajectory-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the relativistic particle model, on top of the\nflat spacetime model of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and the SI unit\nconventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], together with\nthe framework's idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks and ideal light\nsignals:\n\n**(i) Mass and momentum.** Every massive classical particle of the model\ncarries a positive constant invariant mass parameter $m>0$ and has\nfour-momentum $P=mU$, where $U$ is the four-velocity of its future timelike\nworldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]],\n[[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]],\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]); $P$ is a vector of $V$\nwhose SI unit is kilogram metre per second. The formula is adopted only for\n$m>0$; the case $m=0$ is not defined by it and is the separate premise\n[[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]]. In particular $\\eta(P,P)=-m^2c^2$\nis a consequence of the four-velocity normalisation, not a separate\nassumption.\n\n**(ii) Observer measurement identification.** Relative to a future unit\nobserver $n$, the model identifies the measured energy of the particle with\n$E_n(P)$, its measured spatial momentum with $q_n(P)$, its kinetic energy with\n$T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ and its rest energy with $mc^2$, where $E_n,q_n,T_n$ are\nthe quantities of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]; the\nunits are the joule, kilogram metre per second and kilogram of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. This clause is a formulation\nconvention identifying mathematical quantities with the quantities the model\ncalls measured; it is not a measurement report and no experiment is claimed.\n\n**(iii) Isolated collision balance.** An isolated collision is represented by\na mass-specified on-shell collision datum at an event, with finite nonempty\nincoming and outgoing lists of the massive particles of (i) and/or the ideal\nmassless particles of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]], and with\nno external four-momentum transfer or omitted leg. The interaction is\nidealized as confined to that event. The sum of the incoming four-momenta equals the sum of the\noutgoing four-momenta, in the sense of the collision data of\n[[def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation]]: the model adopts\n$Q_{\\mathrm{in}}=Q_{\\mathrm{out}}$ for such an isolated datum. No particular\ncollision is asserted to occur, and no mechanism, probability, cross section\nor amplitude is supplied by this clause.\n\n**(iv) Equation of motion for a stipulated force.** Put\n$H_c^+:=\\{U\\in C^+:\\eta(U,U)=-c^2\\}$. A stipulated force law is a given\nmap $F:D\\to V$ on a given subset $D\\subseteq A\\times H_c^+$,\nwith force components in $\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s^2$. No regularity\naway from the specified worldline is assumed. Along a given $C^2$ massive worldline\n$x:I\\to A$ with $(x(s),U(s))\\in D$ for all $s\\in I$, the model's particle\nequation is $m\\,dU/d\\tau=F(x,U)$, with\n$dU/d\\tau$ the proper-time derivative of\n[[def-sr-proper-time-derivative-and-four-force]], so that the stipulated $F$\nis the four-force of the motion. A particular force law, and the existence,\nuniqueness and domain of solutions of the resulting equation, are not adopted\nhere and belong to a separate model.\n\nEmpirical support for these assumptions is a nonlogical relation of the\nframework, not a proof; the idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks,\nideal light signals and flat spacetime are inherited and travel to every\nconsumer of this premise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-em-minkowski-physical-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of this framework:\n\n(i) The oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ used by\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]], together with the\nconversion constant $c>0$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]], is the spacetime of the\nframework: its points represent idealized physical events, displacements\nrepresent idealized spacetime separations, and the causal classification of the\nmodel is the framework's causal structure. In the concrete coordinate\nrealization [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]], $A=V=\\mathbb R^4$ and\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. That supplier uses the symbol $C^+$ for\nthe nonzero future causal cone, including null vectors; here, as in the\nspecial-relativity model, $C^+$ denotes its future timelike subset. The\nfuture null boundary vectors retain their separate causal classification.\n\n(ii) The inertial frames and charts of\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] are the inertial descriptions of the\nframework, and changes of description are the proper orthochronous isometries\nadopted in [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]].\n\n(iii) The ideal clock reading rule along future timelike curves and the\nidentification of ideal vacuum light rays with future null straight lines, both\nwith the same constant $c$, are those adopted in\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]].\n\n(iv) The laboratory description of fields and sources is the restricted\ninertial chart of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with the spatial Euclidean structure\nand the SI conventions of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\nStatus and scope: the mathematical model defines the geometry and its theorems\nprove geometric facts only; that physical events are described by the model,\nthat ideal clocks read the stated proper time and that ideal vacuum light\nfollows null straight lines are adopted assumptions, not deductions from\nexperiment or from the mathematical axioms. The special-relativity postulates\nadopted here do not by themselves select the Maxwell equations, a force law or\nany matter model: the electromagnetic equations and couplings of this framework\nare further postulates of it. The framework is classical, flat and non-quantum;\ngravitational curvature, quantum fields and measurement postulates are outside\nits scope.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-em-lorentz-coupling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical postulates of this framework, on the spacetime and particle\nmodel of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]] and\n[[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]] and on the special-relativity particle\nmodel [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]]:\n\n(i) [Test charge in a prescribed external field] Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ be a\nconstant charge parameter ([[def-em-sources]]), $m>0$ an externally supplied\nrest mass, $x:J\\to A$ a future timelike $C^2$ massive worldline with\nfour-velocity $U$, and let $F$ be a prescribed field of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] that is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of the graph\nof $x$ and is generated externally to the particle. Adopt the equation of\nmotion\n\n$$m\\,\\frac{dU}{d\\tau}=q\\,\\Phi(U),$$\n\nwhere, at each original parameter $s\\in J$, $\\Phi=\\Phi_{x(s)}$ is the\nraised-index operator of the field at $x(s)$, defined by\n$\\eta(\\Phi_{x(s)}(u),w)=F(x(s))(w,u)$\n([[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]]); equivalently\n$m\\,dU^\\mu/d\\tau=q\\,F^{\\mu\\nu}U_\\nu$. Here $dU/d\\tau$ is the derivative of $U$\nwith respect to its proper-time function $\\tau(s)$: explicitly\n$dU/d\\tau=U'(s)/\\tau'(s)$, with\n$\\tau'(s)=c^{-1}\\sqrt{-\\eta(x'(s),x'(s))}>0$. It is the four-acceleration of the worldline\n([[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]],\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]). The particle's own field\nis excluded from $F$: this is the external-field coupling, and it defines no\nself-force.\n\n(ii) [Continuum matter coupling] For a smooth source $(\\rho,J)$ of\n[[def-em-sources]] interacting with the field through an explicitly stated\nmatter model to which this coupling applies, adopt the force density\n$\\rho E+J\\times B$ (unit N m$^{-3}$) and the power density $J\\cdot E$ (unit\nW m$^{-3}$) as the electromagnetic source terms of that matter model, with the\nspatial cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; they describe the local\ninteraction of the field with matter and are not a property of a prescribed\ncurrent alone.\n\n(iii) [Scope] The rest mass and the charge are inputs; $m$ is not derived within\nelectromagnetism. The coupling is adopted for constant $q$ and $m>0$; no\nmagnetic-monopole coupling, no radiation reaction and no self-force are\nincluded. In particular the nonrelativistic coordinate statement\n$mr''=q(E+r'\\times B)$ is not adopted as exact: the adopted statement is the\ncovariant one of clause (i), and any low-speed statement requires its own\napproximation hypotheses and error bounds.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let the laboratory region, its inertial chart and the fields and sources be those\nof [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]], with the\nunits of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; fix the ordered basis\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the chart and the oriented volume form $\\varepsilon$ of\n[[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]], with $\\varepsilon_{0123}=+1$ and\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$.\n\n(a) [Components] For a field pair $(E,B)$ of class $C^k$, $k\\ge1$, define the\ncovariant components of the **electromagnetic two-form** $F$ in the chart by\n$F_{00}=0$, $F_{0i}:=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}:=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$\n($i,j,k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$) and $F_{ab}:=-F_{ba}$, the index-raised components being\n$F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. Here\n$\\epsilon_{ijk}$ is the spatial permutation symbol of\n[[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]]. Then $F$ is a $C^k$ type-$(0,2)$\ntensor field on $U$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]], alternating at every event, and the\ncomponents above are exactly its component functions: at $p\\in U$ the tensor\n$F(p)$ is the bilinear form $F(p)(u,w)=F_{ab}(p)u^aw^b$ on $V$ in the\ncoordinates $u^a,w^b$ of $u,w$ in $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$. Indeed each $F_{ab}$ is\na finite sum of products of the $C^k$ functions $E_i,B_k$ of the chart\ncoordinates with the constants $c,\\epsilon_{ijk}$, hence a $C^k$ function of\n$x(p)$, and the component criterion of\n[[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]] applies.\n\n(b) [Observer decomposition] Let $n:=e_0$ be the future unit timelike vector of\nthe chart and let $e_a:=F_{ab}n^b$, $b_a:=(*F)_{ab}n^b$ be the observer\ncomponents of [[thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition]]; then\n$e_i=+E_i/c$, $b_i=-B_i$ and $e_0=b_0=0$, and the invariants and square wedge\nof that theorem read\n\n$$\\tfrac12F_{ab}F^{ab}=|B|^2-|E|^2/c^2,\\qquad F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}=+4\\,(E\\cdot B)/c,\\qquad F\\wedge F=-2\\,(E\\cdot B)\\,\\varepsilon/c,$$\n\nwhere $|\\cdot|$ and $\\cdot$ are the spatial Euclidean structure of the frame.\n(For the same pair $(E,B)$, that theorem's optional clause (d) uses\n$F_{\\rm optional}^{i0}=E_i/c$ and $F_{\\rm optional}^{ij}=-\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, so\n$F_{\\rm optional}=-F$ in all six components, not only the magnetic block. This\nframework fixes clause (a) and uses the theorem's general algebra; its optional\nSI dictionary must be converted by negating the entire tensor.)\n\n(c) [Potentials when they exist] If $\\varphi$ and $A$ are $C^1$ with\n$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$, and\n$A^\\mu:=(\\varphi/c,A)$, $A_\\mu:=\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}A^\\nu$, then\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$; conversely, if a $C^1$\ncovector field $A_\\mu$ on $U$ satisfies $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu\n-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, then $E,B$ recovered from $F$ by clause (a) obey\n$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ with\n$\\varphi:=-cA_0$ and the spatial potential vector $A:=(A_1,A_2,A_3)$. Here\n$\\nabla$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ are those of\n[[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] in the spatial coordinates,\nand the spatial cross-product conventions of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] are\nused. The definition does not assert that every field pair admits such a\npotential; where a $C^1$ potential represents the $C^1$-or-better field of\nclause (a), the field is closed in the explicit sense\n$\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}\n+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0$. This conclusion does not require second\nclassical derivatives of the potential: put\n$H_{\\lambda\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}\n+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}$, which is continuous because $F$ is $C^1$; for\nevery smooth compactly supported test $\\psi$, substituting $F=dA$ and\nintegrating by parts twice, moving the derivatives first onto $\\psi$\n([[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]] (b)) and then off the\npotential factors after commuting only the smooth test derivatives\n([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]), gives $\\int H\\psi=0$; the\nfundamental lemma (a) of [[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]\nthen gives $H=0$. The framework's potential theorems establish existence only\nunder their stated hypotheses; [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]\nsupplies the coordinate derivatives $\\partial_\\mu$ used throughout.\n\n(d) [Four-current and index-raising operator] With the adopted four-current\n$j=(c\\rho,J)$ of [[def-em-sources]], the raised-index operator $\\Phi$ of\n[[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]], defined pointwise on $V$ by\n$\\eta(\\Phi(u),w)=F(w,u)$, satisfies\n$\\Phi(u)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$; the components of $F$\ncarry the tesla (T), those of $j$ carry A m$^{-2}$, and those of a\nfour-potential $A^\\mu$ carry T m.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-fields",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$, its spatial Euclidean structure\nand its units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and\n[[def-em-si-conventions]], and let $k\\ge1$. An **electric field of class\n$C^k$** on the region is a map $E:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ that is of class\n$C^k$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]; a\n**magnetic field of class $C^k$** is a map $B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of\nthe same class. At fixed $t\\in I$ the maps $E(\\cdot,t),B(\\cdot,t):\\Omega\\to\n\\mathbb R^3$ are real spatial vector fields on the region $\\Omega$, read in the\n$\\delta$-orthonormal basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the frame. The framework uses:\n$C^1$ fields in the classical Maxwell branch, $C^2$ fields where second or\nmixed time-space derivatives of the fields appear, and $C^\\infty$ fields in\nthe smooth existence branches; every statement carries its own regularity and\nno regularity beyond it is asserted.\n\nConventions:\n\n(a) The **polar-vector** and **axial-vector** conventions mean the following\ncomponent rules under a spatial orthogonal coordinate change $y'=Qy$, where\n$Q$ is a real $3\\times3$ matrix with $Q^{\\mathsf T}Q=I_3$. Transpose,\nmatrix product and identity are those of [[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]] and\n[[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], and $\\det Q$ is the determinant\nof [[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]]. At corresponding\npoints $E'(y',t)=QE(y,t)$ and $B'(y',t)=(\\det Q)QB(y,t)$. Thus polar and axial\ncomponents agree in their transformation rule when $\\det Q=1$ and differ by\na sign when $\\det Q=-1$. These rules specify the orientation convention;\nthe adopted inertial descriptions have positive orientation, and no physical\norientation-reversing change of description is adopted here.\n\n(b) The fields are defined by their values on $\\Omega\\times I$, and no value at\na point of singular source support is asserted.\n\n(c) $E$ carries V m$^{-1}$ and $B$ carries T.\n\n(d) The identification of the pair $(E,B)$ with the components of an\nalternating two-form, including its transformation behaviour, is made in the\nframework's electromagnetic two-form definition on this page and is not\npresupposed here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-si-conventions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Adopt the SI system of units for this framework, with the following quantity\ntable and conventions; every symbol is a quantity of this framework and the\nlaboratory structure is that of [[def-em-lab-geometry]].\n\n(a) [Coordinates and constants] $x^0=ct$ and each spatial coordinate $x^i$\ncarries the metre (m); the time coordinate $t$ carries the second (s); the\nconversion constant $c>0$ carries m s$^{-1}$ and has the exact SI defining\nvalue $c=299\\,792\\,458\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; the elementary charge $e$ has the\nexact SI defining value $e=1.602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{C}$, and this\nitem does not assert that physical charge is quantised; the magnetic constant\n$\\mu_0>0$ carries N A$^{-2}$ and is measured, so no exact numerical value of\n$\\mu_0$ is asserted. The pre-2019 SI fixed the historical nominal\n$\\mu_{0,\\mathrm{nom}}:=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}\n=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ exactly; since the revised SI took\neffect on 20 May 2019, $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined. The SI brochure\nquotes the nominal $\\mu_{0,\\mathrm{nom}}$ as the estimate at the 2019 adoption\nwith relative standard uncertainty $2.3\\times10^{-10}$; this is historical\nestimate and uncertainty context, not an exact equality for the actual $\\mu_0$\nor a current measured value. The electric constant is defined within the\nframework by\n\n$$\\varepsilon_0:=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$$\n\nin F m$^{-1}$, consistent with the exact SI relation $\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1/c^2$,\nso that $c^2\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1$ and $\\varepsilon_0$ inherits the relative\nstandard uncertainty of $\\mu_0$.\n\n(b) [Electromagnetic quantities] Charge $q$ carries the coulomb (C); the\ncharge density $\\rho$ of the framework's source definition carries C m$^{-3}$\nand the current density $J$ carries A m$^{-2}$, so that the four-current\ncomponents $(c\\rho,J)$ carry A m$^{-2}$; the electric field $E$ carries\nV m$^{-1}$; the magnetic field $B$ carries the tesla (T), where\n$1\\ \\mathrm{T}=1\\ \\mathrm{N\\,A^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$; the scalar potential $\\varphi$\ncarries V and the vector potential $A$ carries T m, so that the four-potential\ncomponents $(\\varphi/c,A)$ carry T m; the components of the electromagnetic\ntwo-form of the framework's tensor definition carry T; a force carries the\nnewton (N), $1\\ \\mathrm{N}=1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}}$.\n\n(c) [Mechanical quantities] A rest mass parameter $m$ carries the kilogram\n(kg) and a four-momentum $P=mU$ of the relativistic particle model carries\nkg m s$^{-1}$; field energy density carries J m$^{-3}$, energy flux\nW m$^{-2}$, so that $1\\ \\mathrm{W}=1\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s^{-1}}=1\\ \\mathrm{V\\,A}$.\n\n(d) [Bookkeeping only] The unit of a quantity records the adopted system of\nunits; it does not show that a quantity is primitive in this or any other\nframework, it contains no measurement, and it does not identify a quantity\nwith a similarly named quantity of another framework. The exact defining\nvalues of $c$ and $e$ fix numerical conventions of the SI; they do not by\nthemselves identify $c$ with the vacuum propagation constant of ideal light,\nwhich is the physical adoption of the framework's Minkowski postulate.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-lab-geometry",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space\n([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial\nframe with inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$\n([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), let $c>0$ be the framework's\nconversion constant, and let $\\delta$ be the Euclidean spatial form of that\nframe on $n^\\perp$, $n:=e_0$, with norm $|\\cdot|$ and positive spatial\norientation ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). Fix a\nnonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ ([[def-interval]]) and a nonempty\nopen set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$, and put\n\n$$U:=\\{p\\in A:\\ \\text{there are }t\\in I,\\ y\\in\\Omega\\text{ with }x^0(p)=ct,\\ (x^1,x^2,x^3)(p)=y\\}.$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) [Chart restriction] $x(U)=\\{(ct,y):t\\in I,\\ y\\in\\Omega\\}$ and\n$\\Phi:U\\to I\\times\\Omega$, $\\Phi(p):=(x^0(p)/c,(x^1,x^2,x^3)(p))$, is a\nbijection, because the inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$ is a bijection and\n$c>0$; its components are the **laboratory time coordinate**\n$t(p):=x^0(p)/c$ and the **laboratory position**\n$r(p):=(x^1(p),x^2(p),x^3(p))$. In the chart coordinates the pair\n$(t(p),r(p))$ is the reading of the laboratory decomposition of $p$. Writing\n$r(p)=(r^1(p),r^2(p),r^3(p))\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the standard basis of\n$\\mathbb R^3$ ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]) labels the three coordinate\ndirections, and the identification $\\mathbb R^3\\cong n^\\perp$ is the linear\nmap $\\sum_{i=1}^3 r^i e_i$ into the three-dimensional real vector space\n$n^\\perp$ ([[def-vector-space]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n\n(b) [Slices and spatial structure] For each $t\\in I$ the slice\n$U_t:=\\{p\\in U:t(p)=t\\}$ is carried bijectively onto $\\Omega$ by $r$; the\nconstant vector $n=e_0$ is future unit timelike, and $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is the\n$\\delta$-orthonormal positively oriented basis of $n^\\perp$ in which the\ncoordinates of $r$ are expressed.\n\n(c) [Laboratory fields] For a nonnegative integer $m$, a **laboratory field of rank $m$** is a map\n$F:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^m$; it is identified with the map $U\\to\\mathbb R^m$,\n$p\\mapsto F(r(p),t(p))$, and with the family $(F(\\cdot,t))_{t\\in I}$ of maps\n$\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^m$, its values at fixed time. Such a field is of class\n$C^k$, for $m\\ge1$, when the map $(y,t)\\mapsto F(y,t)$ is $C^k$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. For $m=0$, set $\\mathbb R^0:=\\{()\\}$;\nthe unique field into this singleton is declared $C^\\infty$ (and $C^k$ for every\nfinite $k$), and each of its partial derivatives is the same singleton-valued\nzero map. For $m\\ge1$ and $k\\ge1$, $\\partial_{y_i}F$ (at fixed\n$t$) and $\\partial_tF$ (at fixed $y$) are the partial derivatives of\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. No regularity of a field is\nasserted beyond the class stated for it.\n\n(d) [Conventions] The product decomposition is a coordinate identification\ninside the single inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$; it is not a Galilean\ntransformation, it does not compare two inertial frames, and it does not\nassert absolute simultaneity. The objects $I,\\Omega,t,r,\\delta$ and the\nidentification $\\mathbb R^3\\cong n^\\perp$ belong to the named frame; changes\nof inertial description are the proper orthochronous maps adopted in\n[[post-sr-inertial-covariance]]. The frame is an origin together with an\nordered basis; it is not a congruence of observers, and no moving observer\nfield is defined here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The particle and light objects of the framework are the following curves and\nvector fields of the affine Minkowski model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]],\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), with the physical interpretation of\n[[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]] and the units of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n(a) [Worldlines] A **worldline** is a regular causal curve $x:J\\to A$ in one of\nthe regularity classes of [[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]] ($J$ an\norder-convex subset of $\\mathbb R$ with at least two elements). A **massive\nworldline** is a $C^k$ future timelike worldline, $k\\ge1$, with everywhere\nnonzero future timelike tangent. A **future null curve** is a $C^1$ curve with\neverywhere nonzero future null tangent. An **ideal vacuum light ray** of the\nadopted physical model follows a null straight line: on its parameter interval\nit has the form $x(s)=p+\\lambda(s)v$, where $p\\in A$, $v\\in V$ is nonzero\nfuture null, and $\\lambda$ is $C^1$ with $\\lambda'(s)>0$. This is the\nstraight-ray adoption of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]]; arbitrary\nfuture null curves are retained as kinematic objects without asserting that\nthey describe vacuum light propagation. Along a null curve the integrand\n$-\\eta(x',x')$ of the proper-time construction\n([[def-sr-proper-time]]) vanishes identically, so that construction yields no\nproper-time parametrization of it, and a light ray is never treated as a\nmassive worldline. A worldline is a curve in spacetime; its reading $r\\circ x$\nin a named laboratory chart is a coordinate description of it, not an object of\nanother observer.\n\n(b) [Four-velocity and four-acceleration] For a massive worldline the\nfour-velocity $U$ and, when it is $C^2$, the four-acceleration $A$ are those of\n[[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]; wherever defined they satisfy\n$\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ and $\\eta(U,A)=0$, by\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n\n(c) [Chart reading] If a massive $C^2$ worldline is described in a laboratory\nchart of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] by $t\\mapsto(ct,r(t))$ on an open interval of\nlaboratory times, then $|r'(t)|<c$ for every $t$, with four-velocity\n$U=\\gamma(c,r')$ and Lorentz factor\n$\\gamma=(1-|r'|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, by clause (e) of\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n\n(d) [Frames and observers] An inertial frame in this framework is the\norigin-with-basis object of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]; a point\nobserver is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in V$ with rest space $n^\\perp$ and\nrest projection $h$, as in clauses (c)-(d) of\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. On an open spacetime region\n$W\\subseteq A$, an **observer congruence field** means a $C^1$ map $n:W\\to V$ with\n$n(p)$ future timelike and $\\eta(n(p),n(p))=-1$ at every $p$; its observer\nhistories, when given, are integral curves satisfying $dx/d\\tau=cn(x)$.\nA **tetrad field** is a tuple of $C^1$ maps $(f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3):W\\to V^4$\nwhose values form a positively oriented $\\eta$-orthonormal basis at every\nevent, with $f_0$ future timelike. Regularity here means componentwise $C^1$\nin inertial coordinates. These fields need not be constant and are distinct\nfrom an inertial origin-with-basis frame, its coordinate chart and a single\npoint observer; no global congruence or tetrad is asserted to exist.\n\n(e) [Scope] No force law, no self-field and no extended-body structure is\nsupplied here; an accelerating massive worldline requires a force model stated\nseparately, and an ideal light ray carries no dispersive, quantum or\npolarisation structure in this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-sources",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let the laboratory region and its chart be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with\nthe units of [[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n(a) [Charge parameter] The framework takes a real **charge parameter**\n$q\\in\\mathbb R$, measured in coulombs, attached to a source; its sign is the\nsign of the source and it is a parameter of the model. Charge quantisation is\nnot asserted.\n\n(b) [Smooth source fields] A **smooth (volume) source field** on\n$\\Omega\\times I$ is a pair $(\\rho,J)$ of maps $\\rho:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$,\n$J:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ that is continuous on $\\Omega\\times I$ and of\nclass $C^1$ wherever a displayed equation differentiates it; $\\rho$ is the\n**frame charge density** (C m$^{-3}$) and $J$ the **frame current density**\n(A m$^{-2}$). For each fixed $t\\in I$ retain the compact Jordan branch: if\n$V\\subseteq\\Omega$ is compact and Jordan measurable, define\n\n$$Q_R(V,t):=\\int_V\\rho(y,t)dy$$\n\nby [[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], with integrability supplied by\n[[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]]. For the\nfull measurable-volume branch assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]),\nas required by the published Lebesgue-measure interface. If $V$ is any bounded\nLebesgue-measurable subset with compact closure $\\overline V\\subseteq\\Omega$,\ndefine\n\n$$Q(V,t):=\\int_V\\rho(y,t)d\\lambda_3(y)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}F_t(y)\\mathbf{1}_V(y)d\\lambda_3(y),$$\n\nwhere $F_t=\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ on $\\Omega$ and $0$ elsewhere; the signed integral is\ndefined through its positive and negative parts\n([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]],\n[[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]). The\nfunction $F_t$ is Borel, and hence Lebesgue measurable: for every open\n$O\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ its preimage inside the open set $\\Omega$ is relatively\nopen in $\\Omega$, hence open in $\\mathbb R^3$, and the closed complement of\n$\\Omega$ is added precisely when $0\\in O$\n([[thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel]],\n[[thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable]]). This integral is absolutely\nfinite: $\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ is bounded on the compact $\\overline V$ by\n[[thm-extreme-value-metric]], $V$ has finite Lebesgue measure by\n[[prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets]], and the\nmonotonicity and homogeneity of the nonnegative integral\n([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]) bound\n$\\int|F_t|\\mathbf{1}_Vd\\lambda_3\\le M_t\\lambda_3(V)<\\infty$, the indicator\nintegral being its simple-function value\n([[prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral]]); the\npositive and negative parts are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]]. On\nthe compact Jordan branch, under the same Countable Choice assumption,\n$Q=Q_R$ by [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]].\nThe\nempty region has charge zero in both branches. The current flux remains\ndefined independently: for each oriented surface patch in $\\Omega$ the current\nflux at time $t$ is the surface integral of $J(\\cdot,t)$ in the induced\norientation, as defined in [[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]].\nPoint sources and sheet sources are not source fields in this sense; they are\ndifferent objects, defined by their own pairings when the framework uses them.\n\n(c) [Covariant identification] Write $j:=(c\\rho,J)$, so $j^0=c\\rho$ and\n$j^i=J^i$. The framework adopts as a model premise the identification of the\ncharge-current of a source with a vector field of the affine Minkowski model,\nand hence the component transformation law of\n[[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] for $j$ between inertial charts.\nThis is a model premise, not a theorem about physical currents.\n\n(d) [Frame density, scalar multiplier and the normalized material branch]\nSuppose additionally that the identified $j$ is $C^1$ and $j=\\rho_0U$, with a\n$C^1$ future timelike vector field $U$ and a $C^1$ real scalar multiplier\n$\\rho_0$. Since $U^0>0$, define the normalized chart direction $u:=U/U^0$, so\n$u^0=1$, $u^i=U^i/U^0$. Then $j=(\\rho_0U^0)u$ and, because the electromagnetic\nconvention is $j^0=c\\rho$,\n\n$$\\rho=\\rho_0U^0/c,\\qquad J^i=\\rho_0U^i=c\\rho\\,u^i.$$\n\nPointwise equality $\\rho=\\rho_0$ holds if and only if $\\rho_0(U^0-c)=0$,\nincluding the zero-multiplier case. Where $\\rho_0\\ne0$, equality is equivalent\nto $U^0=c$, which does not alone imply $U^i=0$ for an arbitrary future timelike\n$U$. Thus a scalar multiplier is not automatically a Lorentz rest charge\ndensity. The direction $u$ is chart normalized, with no unit or\nproper-four-velocity normalization imposed in this general branch; if its\ncoordinate velocity interpretation is adopted, the spatial velocity is\n$v^i:=cu^i$.\n\nFor the separate convective source model, explicitly assume that $U$ is the\nmaterial proper-time four-velocity, normalized by $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ as in\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. This is an additional model\ncondition, not a consequence of the factorization $j=\\rho_0U$. In that branch,\n$U=\\gamma(c,v)$, $|v|<c$, $\\gamma=(1-|v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, and\n\n$$\\rho=\\gamma\\rho_0,\\qquad J=\\rho v.$$\n\nHere $\\rho_0$ is the rest charge density of this stipulated convective model:\nin a comoving inertial frame $U=(c,0,0,0)$ and $j=(c\\rho_0,0,0,0)$. The\nequality $\\rho=\\rho_0$ then holds exactly where $\\rho_0=0$ or $v=0$\n(equivalently $\\gamma=1$). Both signs of charge and the zero-density case are\nretained.\n\n(e) [Scope] This definition fixes the source objects of the smooth branch and\ntheir transformation interface. It asserts no conservation law (that is proved\nfor stated source classes) and no regularity of singular sources.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the framework's spacetime, field and worldline objects of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]], [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]], with the SI units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and clause (i) of the adopted Lorentz coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]]: a constant charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ of [[def-em-sources]] (a), an externally supplied rest mass $m>0$, a future timelike $C^2$ massive worldline $x:J\\to A$ with four-velocity $U$, and a prescribed field $F$ of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] that is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of the graph of $x$ and is generated externally to the particle; the adopted equation of motion is $$m\\,\\frac{dU}{d\\tau}=q\\,\\Phi(U),\\qquad \\eta(\\Phi(u),w)=F(w,u),$$ $U$ the four-velocity of [[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]] (b) with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $t\\mapsto r(t)$ be the laboratory chart reading of the worldline, $v:=r'$ its coordinate velocity and $\\gamma:=(1-|v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, so that $U=\\gamma(c,v)$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (e)). Then, wherever the worldline is $C^2$: (a) [Coordinate force and power] with the coordinate three-momentum $p:=m\\gamma v$ of [[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]] (a), $$\\frac{dp}{dt}=q\\,(E+v\\times B),\\qquad \\frac{d(\\gamma mc^2)}{dt}=q\\,E\\cdot v,$$ the derivatives taken with respect to the chart time $t$ and $E,B$ the components of $F$ in the laboratory frame. (b) [Norm preservation] $s\\mapsto\\eta(U(s),U(s))$ is constant on $J$ with value $-c^2$; in particular a solution with future timelike initial data remains future timelike, and $|v(t)|<c$ wherever the reading is differentiable ([[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]]). (c) [Controlled low-speed expansion] for every $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, on any interval where $|v|/c\\le\\delta$, $$|\\gamma-1|\\le\\frac{|v|^2/c^2}{2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad \\Bigl|\\gamma-1-\\frac{|v|^2}{2c^2}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\delta^2)^{-5/2}\\Bigl(\\frac{|v|^2}{c^2}\\Bigr)^{2},$$ and consequently $$|p-mv|\\le m|v|\\,\\frac{|v|^2}{2c^2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad \\Bigl|\\gamma mc^2-mc^2-\\tfrac12m|v|^2\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\delta^2)^{-5/2}\\frac{m|v|^4}{c^2};$$ the constants depend only on $\\delta$, so the bounds are uniform in $|v|/c\\le\\delta$. These are pointwise algebraic expansions; they assert no approximation of the trajectory over a time interval, which would require a separate stability argument. (d) [Scope] The charge is constant, $m>0$ is an externally supplied parameter and $F$ is a prescribed exterior field: the particle's own field, radiation reaction and any self-force are excluded, as are magnetic monopoles and zero or variable mass; the nonrelativistic coordinate law is not adopted as exact, and (c) is a controlled kinematic expansion, not a trajectory approximation. The statement is conditional on the adopted postulates and makes no empirical claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "pthm-rpm-inverse-momentum-and-work",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, and let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future-timelike worldline in the flat Minkowski model of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. Use one inertial frame and parameterize the curve by that chart's time coordinate $t$, so its coordinates are $(ct,\\mathbf x(t))$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Identify the observer rest space with $\\mathbb R^3$ by the frame's spatial orthonormal basis and use its Euclidean dot product and norm ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). Write $\\mathbf v=d\\mathbf x/dt$, $\\gamma=(1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, proper time $\\tau$, and $U=dx/d\\tau$. In the constant-mass sector of [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]], let $m>0$ be constant and $P=mU$. In this chart write $P=(E/c,\\mathbf p)$, where $E$ and $\\mathbf p$ are the observer energy and spatial momentum of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]. Then $|\\mathbf v|<c$, $E=\\gamma mc^2>0$, $\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v$, and\n\n$$E^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2,\\qquad \\mathbf v=\\frac{c^2\\mathbf p}{E}.$$\n\nDefine the three-force by $\\mathbf F:=d\\mathbf p/dt$ and the four-force by $K:=dP/d\\tau$ in this flat inertial chart. In SI units $m$ is in kilograms, $c$ and $\\mathbf v$ in metres per second, $E$ in joules, $\\mathbf p$ in kilogram metres per second, and $\\mathbf F,K$ in newtons. Then\n\n$$\\frac{dE}{dt}=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v,\\qquad K=\\gamma\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v}{c},\\mathbf F\\right).$$\n\nSeparately, let $I_\\tau\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open proper-time interval, let $m:I_\\tau\\to(0,\\infty)$ be $C^1$, and let $U:I_\\tau\\to V$ be a $C^1$ future timelike vector field with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Measure $m$ in kilograms and $dm/d\\tau$ in kilograms per second, and set $P(\\tau):=m(\\tau)U(\\tau)$. With $K:=dP/d\\tau$, the kinematic ansatz satisfies\n\n$$\\eta(P,K)=-m\\,\\frac{dm}{d\\tau}\\,c^2.$$\n\nThe last identity is only a differentiated mass-shell relation; it prescribes no evolution for $m$. None of these conclusions assumes a force law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "lem-rpm-finite-time-ode-comparison",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, $T\\ge0$, $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and $K\\subseteq U$ be compact and convex. Let $F_\\epsilon,F_0:[0,T]\\times U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous. Suppose that on $[0,T]\\times K$ both fields are bounded in the Euclidean norm by $B\\ge0$, the comparison field $F_0$ is state-Lipschitz with constant $L\\ge0$, and\n\n$$\\|F_\\epsilon(t,z)-F_0(t,z)\\|_2\\le\\eta\\qquad(t\\in[0,T],\\ z\\in K)$$\n\nfor some $\\eta\\ge0$. Let $z_\\epsilon,z_0\\in C^1([0,T],\\mathbb R^n)$ solve $\\dot z_\\epsilon=F_\\epsilon(t,z_\\epsilon)$ and $\\dot z_0=F_0(t,z_0)$, with both trajectories contained in $K$. Then, for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n\n$$\\|z_\\epsilon(t)-z_0(t)\\|_2\\le\\bigl(\\|z_\\epsilon(0)-z_0(0)\\|_2+\\eta t\\bigr)e^{Lt}.$$\n\nIn particular, equal initial states give $\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|z_\\epsilon(t)-z_0(t)\\|_2\\le\\eta T e^{LT}$. The field bound $B$ alone is not a stability hypothesis; the comparison-field Lipschitz bound $L$ and uniform field error $\\eta$ are part of the assumptions. No existence through $T$ follows unless the trajectories are known to exist there.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-rpm-low-speed-remainders",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,c>0$, let $0<\\delta<1$, and let $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$ have Euclidean norm satisfying $|v|/c\\le\\delta$. Put\n\n$$z:=\\frac{|v|^2}{c^2},\\qquad \\gamma:=(1-z)^{-1/2},\\qquad p:=m\\gamma v,\\qquad E:=\\gamma mc^2.$$\n\nThen\n\n$$0\\le\\gamma-1\\le\\frac{z}{2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad 0\\le\\gamma-1-\\frac z2\\le\\frac{3z^2}{8(1-\\delta^2)^{5/2}},$$\n\nand\n\n$$|p-mv|\\le\\frac{m|v|^3}{2c^2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad 0\\le E-mc^2-\\frac12m|v|^2\\le\\frac{3m|v|^4}{8c^2(1-\\delta^2)^{5/2}}.$$\n\nIf assigned SI kinematic units, $m$ is in kilograms, $c,v$ in metres per second, $p$ in kilogram metres per second and $E$ in joules. These are pointwise algebraic inequalities; they assume no equation of motion or empirical input.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $u=(u_x,u_y,u_z)$ and $v=(v_x,v_y,v_z)$ in $\\mathbb R^3$, define $u\\times v=(u_yv_z-u_zv_y,\\,u_zv_x-u_xv_z,\\,u_xv_y-u_yv_x)$.\n\nThis is the right-handed **cross product**. The displayed coordinates are those of the standard basis ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]), and inner products and norms are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
          "claim": "The stated future-timelike $C^2$ worldline has four-velocity $U_c=\\gamma_c(c,\\dot x_c)$ and mechanical momentum $p_c=m\\gamma_c\\dot x_c$. By [L1], its spatial momentum obeys $\\dot p_c=q[\\mathbf E(t,x_c)+\\dot x_c\\times\\mathbf B(t,x_c)]$. By [L2], $\\mathcal E_c=c\\sqrt{m^2c^2+|p_c|^2}$ and $\\dot x_c=c^2p_c/\\mathcal E_c=p_c/[m\\sqrt{1+|p_c|^2/(m^2c^2)}]=V_c(p_c)$. Thus the relativistic worldline coordinates satisfy $\\dot z_c=G_c(t,z_c)$, while $z_N$ satisfies the declared Newtonian comparison equation by hypothesis.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
