    "pthm-rpm-prescribed-force-local-motion": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a named massive test body with constant primitive mass $m>0$ and momentum $P=mU$ as defined in [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]], one may adopt a specified smooth force field $f(x,V)$ on an open neighborhood of the future unit timelike shell within the future timelike velocity cone and the equation\n\n$$D_\\tau P=f(x,U),\\qquad g(U,f)=0.$$\n\nThe orthogonality condition is part of this model's compatibility assumptions: it preserves $g(U,U)=-c^2$ for constant $m$. It is not a law selecting a force for every particle or interaction. A local IVP follows only where the named force field is smooth, as in [[lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp]]. The force has SI units newtons when $\\tau$ is in seconds and $P$ has units kg m s$^{-1}$. No empirical force measurements, universal coupling, or global existence are asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the signature $(-,+,+,+)$ throughout. In the flat case, **Minkowski spacetime** means the affine model $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]: $A$ is an affine space, $\\eta$ is a nondegenerate Lorentz form on its four-dimensional displacement space, and $C^+$ selects the future cone. In the curved case, a **background spacetime** means a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]; the metric and time orientation are fixed data, not dynamical unknowns here. No field equation or completeness assumption is included.\n\nA **massive worldline** on an open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a $C^2$ curve $x:I\\to M$ with future-directed timelike tangent $T=dx/ds$ at every parameter value. This is the smooth subclass of [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]. A **null worldline** is a $C^2$ curve with nonzero future-directed null tangent everywhere. Its tangent has no proper-time normalization: $g(T,T)=0$, so the massive proper-time formula does not apply. In the flat model these are affine curves into $A$ with tangent in the selected future timelike or null cone from [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]].\n\nAn **observer at an event** $p\\in M$ is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in T_pM$ with $g_p(n,n)=-1$, as in [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]. Its instantaneous rest space is $n^\\perp=\\{v:g_p(v,n)=0\\}$. This is a pointwise vector-space construction; the definition does not assign a clock reading or a moving frame to a lone observer.\n\nAn **observer congruence** on an open set $U\\subseteq M$ is a smooth field of future unit timelike vectors on $U$, as in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]; its observer curves are its integral curves. A **tetrad** is a chosen orthonormal frame field, as in [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. A **chart** is a coordinate map; an adapted chart is a chart whose spatial coordinates are constant along the congruence's integral curves. These objects are distinct: a chart need not be inertial, a tetrad need not be coordinate induced, and a congruence is a field of observers rather than a chart or a frame. This distinction agrees with [[def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction]].\n\nThe coordinates and units used later are SI: in an inertial chart $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ are metres, $t$ is seconds, $c>0$ is metres per second, and proper time is measured in seconds. In a general chart, coordinate components inherit their coordinate units; invariant equations are tensorial. No global inertial chart, global tetrad, or global observer congruence is asserted to exist.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the massive and null sectors as separately adopted in [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] and [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]]. For a massive body of constant mass $m>0$, its **mechanical four-momentum** is $P=mU$, where $U$ is its future unit tangent with $g(U,U)=-c^2$. In the null sector, the mechanical four-momentum is the separately specified nonzero future-null vector $P$ from the einbein formulation; no null proper time or $P=mU$ is defined.\n\nAt an event, for any observer $n$ of [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]], define the spatial momentum and observer energy by\n\n$$q_n(P):=P+g(P,n)n\\in n^\\perp,\\qquad E_n(P):=-c\\,g(P,n).$$\n\nThe projection identity and positive-definiteness of $n^\\perp$ are supplied by [[lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition]]. With SI units, $P$ has units kg m s$^{-1}$, $q_n$ has the same units, and $E_n$ has units joules. This definition is pointwise and does not assert conservation, positivity, or an energy interpretation for an arbitrary vector field that is not an observer.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold and let $m,c>0$. Let $\\mathcal V\\subset TM$ be an open set of future timelike vectors and let $f$ be a smooth vector field along the bundle projection $\\mathcal V\\to M$, with $f(p,V)\\in T_pM$ and $g_p(V,f(p,V))=0$. For every initial datum $(p_0,U_0)\\in\\mathcal V$ satisfying $g_{p_0}(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$, there is a unique local curve $(x,U)$ through it satisfying $D_sx=U$ and $D_sU=f(x,U)/m$. The solution depends smoothly on its initial data and on any finite-dimensional parameters on which $f$ depends smoothly, on a common sufficiently short compact interval near the base datum. It remains on the future unit timelike shell. There is a unique maximal solution on an open interval, but this interval need not be all of $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-rpm-shell-force-ivp",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^8$ be an open coordinate-state domain with state $z=(x,U)\\in\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4$, and let $G:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R^8$ be the smooth autonomous coordinate vector field of the shell-tangent force system in [[lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp]]. Let $z:(a,b)\\to\\mathcal D$ be its unique maximal solution. If $b<\\infty$ and there are $b_0\\in(a,b)$ and a compact set $K\\subset\\mathcal D$ such that $z([b_0,b))\\subseteq K$, then the solution extends beyond $b$, contradicting maximality. The analogous statement holds at a finite left endpoint. Therefore a finite maximal endpoint can occur only if the state eventually leaves every compact subset of its open coordinate-state domain.\n\nThis is a mathematical continuation criterion for the coordinate ODE. It asserts neither that a solution exists for all proper times nor that the background spacetime is geodesically complete.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
          "claim": "The prescribed smooth background and future timelike worldline use the conventions of [F1]. Since $m$ is constant, [F2] gives $D_\\tau P=mD_\\tau U$. Substituting [A1] yields $D_\\tau U=f(x,U)/m$, with the force smooth on the open velocity domain and orthogonal to $U$. These are exactly the hypotheses of [L1], so for each stated initial datum there is a unique local solution; [L1] also proves preservation of the future unit timelike shell.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2.1",
          "claim": "The unique local solutions patch by uniqueness to the maximal open-interval solution supplied by [L1]. At either finite endpoint, if the coordinate state remained in a compact subset of a chart-state domain on which the force vector field is smooth, [L2] would extend that solution, contradicting maximality. Therefore every finite maximal endpoint leaves every such compact subset.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "L1",
            "L2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The Statement begins with a fixed initial datum in the future timelike domain; it makes no claim for an empty initial-data set."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The permitted zero-force case reduces to D_tau U=0 in step 1.1 and is covered by local shell existence."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem follows a single named massive test body and makes no count-dependent claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 preserves the normalized future shell for the zero-force special case; the proof does not divide by force magnitude."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 handles both finite interval endpoints by compact-containment continuation; no endpoint is asserted to be infinite."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Uniqueness supplies one local solution; no choice from a family of solutions is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem is an existence and continuation assertion, not an equivalence."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem is an existence and continuation assertion, not an equivalence."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "Take the permitted special case f=0.",
          "result": "The adopted equation reduces to D_tau U=0; the local shell theorem still supplies a future unit timelike solution, and the continuation claim remains conditional on compact chart-state containment.",
          "verifies": "The force identification in step 1.1 and continuation argument in step 2.1."
        }
      ]
    },
    "pthm-rpm-controlled-newtonian-reduction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the relativistic particle model, on top of the\nflat spacetime model of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and the SI unit\nconventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], together with\nthe framework's idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks and ideal light\nsignals:\n\n**(i) Mass and momentum.** Every massive classical particle of the model\ncarries a positive constant invariant mass parameter $m>0$ and has\nfour-momentum $P=mU$, where $U$ is the four-velocity of its future timelike\nworldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]],\n[[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]],\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]); $P$ is a vector of $V$\nwhose SI unit is kilogram metre per second. The formula is adopted only for\n$m>0$; the case $m=0$ is not defined by it and is the separate premise\n[[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]]. In particular $\\eta(P,P)=-m^2c^2$\nis a consequence of the four-velocity normalisation, not a separate\nassumption.\n\n**(ii) Observer measurement identification.** Relative to a future unit\nobserver $n$, the model identifies the measured energy of the particle with\n$E_n(P)$, its measured spatial momentum with $q_n(P)$, its kinetic energy with\n$T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ and its rest energy with $mc^2$, where $E_n,q_n,T_n$ are\nthe quantities of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]; the\nunits are the joule, kilogram metre per second and kilogram of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. This clause is a formulation\nconvention identifying mathematical quantities with the quantities the model\ncalls measured; it is not a measurement report and no experiment is claimed.\n\n**(iii) Isolated collision balance.** An isolated collision is represented by\na mass-specified on-shell collision datum at an event, with finite nonempty\nincoming and outgoing lists of the massive particles of (i) and/or the ideal\nmassless particles of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]], and with\nno external four-momentum transfer or omitted leg. The interaction is\nidealized as confined to that event. The sum of the incoming four-momenta equals the sum of the\noutgoing four-momenta, in the sense of the collision data of\n[[def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation]]: the model adopts\n$Q_{\\mathrm{in}}=Q_{\\mathrm{out}}$ for such an isolated datum. No particular\ncollision is asserted to occur, and no mechanism, probability, cross section\nor amplitude is supplied by this clause.\n\n**(iv) Equation of motion for a stipulated force.** Put\n$H_c^+:=\\{U\\in C^+:\\eta(U,U)=-c^2\\}$. A stipulated force law is a given\nmap $F:D\\to V$ on a given subset $D\\subseteq A\\times H_c^+$,\nwith force components in $\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s^2$. No regularity\naway from the specified worldline is assumed. Along a given $C^2$ massive worldline\n$x:I\\to A$ with $(x(s),U(s))\\in D$ for all $s\\in I$, the model's particle\nequation is $m\\,dU/d\\tau=F(x,U)$, with\n$dU/d\\tau$ the proper-time derivative of\n[[def-sr-proper-time-derivative-and-four-force]], so that the stipulated $F$\nis the four-force of the motion. A particular force law, and the existence,\nuniqueness and domain of solutions of the resulting equation, are not adopted\nhere and belong to a separate model.\n\nEmpirical support for these assumptions is a nonlogical relation of the\nframework, not a proof; the idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks,\nideal light signals and flat spacetime are inherited and travel to every\nconsumer of this premise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the massive and null sectors as separately adopted in [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] and [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]]. For a massive body of constant mass $m>0$, its **mechanical four-momentum** is $P=mU$, where $U$ is its future unit tangent with $g(U,U)=-c^2$. In the null sector, the mechanical four-momentum is the separately specified nonzero future-null vector $P$ from the einbein formulation; no null proper time or $P=mU$ is defined.\n\nAt an event, for any observer $n$ of [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]], define the spatial momentum and observer energy by\n\n$$q_n(P):=P+g(P,n)n\\in n^\\perp,\\qquad E_n(P):=-c\\,g(P,n).$$\n\nThe projection identity and positive-definiteness of $n^\\perp$ are supplied by [[lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition]]. With SI units, $P$ has units kg m s$^{-1}$, $q_n$ has the same units, and $E_n$ has units joules. This definition is pointwise and does not assert conservation, positivity, or an energy interpretation for an arbitrary vector field that is not an observer.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-rpm-low-speed-uniform-remainders",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C>0$, $0<\\varepsilon_*$, and $\\rho:=C^2\\varepsilon_*<1$. For $0<\\varepsilon\\le\\varepsilon_*$, $m,c>0$, and $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $|v|\\le C\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}\\,c$, put $z=|v|^2/c^2$, $\\gamma=(1-z)^{-1/2}$, $p=m\\gamma v$, and $E=\\gamma mc^2$. Then $z\\le\\rho$ and\n\n$$0\\le\\gamma-1\\le\\frac{C^2\\varepsilon}{2(1-\\rho)^{3/2}},\\qquad 0\\le\\gamma-1-\\frac z2\\le\\frac{3C^4\\varepsilon^2}{8(1-\\rho)^{5/2}},$$\n\n$$|p-mv|\\le\\frac{mC^3c\\,\\varepsilon^{3/2}}{2(1-\\rho)^{3/2}},\\qquad 0\\le E-mc^2-\\frac12m|v|^2\\le\\frac{3mC^4c^2\\varepsilon^2}{8(1-\\rho)^{5/2}}.$$\n\nFor the formal coordinate-time Lagrangian densities\n\n$$\\mathcal L_c(v):=-mc^2\\sqrt{1-|v|^2/c^2},\\qquad \\mathcal L_N(v):=\\frac12m|v|^2,$$\n\none also has\n\n$$|\\mathcal L_c(v)+mc^2-\\mathcal L_N(v)|\\le\\frac{mC^4c^2\\varepsilon^2}{8(1-\\rho)^{3/2}}.$$\n\nSubtracting the constant rest-energy density $-mc^2$ changes an action on a fixed coordinate-time interval only by a path-independent constant. These are uniform algebraic estimates for the stipulated family; they assert no equation of motion or trajectory existence.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a spinless, structureless massive test body of constant primitive mass $m>0$ on a prescribed background satisfying [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]], adopt the action\n\n$$S_m[x]=-mc\\int\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds=-mc^2\\int d\\tau,$$\n\nfor future timelike curves, with fixed-endpoint stationary paths as the model's free trajectories. When $s$ is measured in seconds, $T$ has units metres per second; in SI units the action has units joule-seconds. The equality is the proper-time change of variable of [[thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization]]. This is an adopted model premise, not a consequence of the metric or clock postulate. It excludes self-gravity, radiation reaction, spin, finite-size structure, particle creation, and quantum effects; it does not assert empirical adequacy for any particular body.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "pthm-rpm-free-massive-and-null-geodesics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "On a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, stationary paths of the adopted massive action of [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] satisfy $D_\\tau U=0$ in proper-time parameter, with $P=mU$ and $m>0$ constant. In the separately adopted null sector of [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]], stationary einbein paths have a nonzero future-null momentum $P$ satisfying $D_\\lambda P=0$ in an affine parameter $\\lambda$ with $dx/d\\lambda=P$. Through each allowed initial point and tangent, each equation has a unique local solution and a unique maximal solution on an open interval. Neither maximal interval is asserted to be all of $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-gr-weak-field-scaled-trajectory-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,K,L,C,\\varepsilon_0,\\varphi,r)$ be stationary weak-field data\nwith spatial base $U=\\pi(\\Omega)$, compact $K\\subseteq U$, spatial potential\n$\\Phi=\\varepsilon c^2\\varphi$ and $c>0$\n([[def-gr-stationary-weak-field-data]]), let $\\varepsilon_*$ and $C_2$ be\nthe threshold and constant of\n[[lem-gr-weak-field-christoffel-bounds]], let $S_*>0$ and\n$0<\\varepsilon\\le\\varepsilon_*$, and put\n$T_\\varepsilon:=LS_*/(\\sqrt\\varepsilon c)$,\n$U_L:=\\{X\\in\\mathbb R^3:LX\\in U\\}$, and\n$\\psi(X):=\\varphi(LX)$ for $X\\in U_L$.\n\nEquip each $\\mathbb R^3$ here with the Euclidean inner product\n$\\langle X,Y\\rangle=\\sum_{i=1}^3X^iY^i$ and norm\n$\\|X\\|_2=\\sqrt{\\langle X,X\\rangle}$\n([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). In the standard coordinates,\n$\\nabla\\psi=(\\partial_{X^1}\\psi,\\partial_{X^2}\\psi,\\partial_{X^3}\\psi)$\n([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]); write\n$D^2\\psi:=D(\\nabla\\psi)$ for its derivative and\n$\\|D^2\\psi(X)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}:=\\sup_{\\|v\\|_2=1}\\|D^2\\psi(X)v\\|_2$.\n\nSuppose:\n\n- **(H1)** there is an affinely parametrized geodesic of $g(\\varepsilon)$ admitting\n  a $C^2$ reparametrization $\\gamma_\\varepsilon$ by $t=x^0/c$ on\n  $[0,T_\\varepsilon]$, with $dt/ds\\ne0$ in its affine parameter $s$,\n  whose length-coordinate components are\n  $\\gamma_\\varepsilon(t)=(ct,x_\\varepsilon(t))\\in\\Omega$, with spatial\n  trajectory $x_\\varepsilon(t)\\in K$ and\n  $|\\dot x_\\varepsilon(t)|\\le C\\sqrt\\varepsilon c$ for every such $t$;\n- **(H2)** setting $t(S):=LS/(\\sqrt\\varepsilon c)$,\n  $X_\\varepsilon(S):=x_\\varepsilon(t(S))/L$ and\n  $W_\\varepsilon(S):=\\dot x_\\varepsilon(t(S))/(\\sqrt\\varepsilon c)$ for\n  $S\\in[0,S_*]$, and letting $K'\\subseteq U_L$ be a compact convex set\n  containing every $X_\\varepsilon(S)$, there is a $C^1$ map\n  $(X_*,W_*):[0,S_*]\\to\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3$ with\n  $X_*([0,S_*])\\subseteq K'$ and\n\n$$X_*'(S)=W_*(S),\\qquad W_*'(S)=-\\nabla\\psi\\bigl(X_*(S)\\bigr)\\qquad(0\\le S\\le S_*);$$\n\n- **(H3)** $\\|D^2\\psi(X)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le K_2$ for every $X\\in K'$, where $K_2\\ge0$.\n\nThen for every $S\\in[0,S_*]$, with\n$D(S):=\\|X_\\varepsilon(S)-X_*(S)\\|_2+\\|W_\\varepsilon(S)-W_*(S)\\|_2$,\n\n$$D(S)\\le\\bigl(D(0)+C_3\\varepsilon S_*\\bigr)\\,e^{(1+K_2)S_*},$$\n\nwhere $C_3$ depends only on $C$, $L$ and the dimension. Consequently the\nfollowing hold.\n\n**(i) Convergence.** If $D(0)\\to0$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ along the\nfamily, then $\\sup_{0\\le S\\le S_*}D(S)\\to0$.\n\n**(ii) Rate for identical data.** If $D(0)=0$, then\n$\\sup_{0\\le S\\le S_*}D(S)\\le C_3\\varepsilon S_*e^{(1+K_2)S_*}=O(\\varepsilon)$.\n\n**(iii) Long coordinate times.** The coordinate-time horizon is\n$T_\\varepsilon=LS_*/(\\sqrt\\varepsilon c)$, so the fixed scaled interval\n$[0,S_*]$ corresponds to coordinate times growing like\n$\\varepsilon^{-1/2}$; the limit statement is therefore not a large-$t$\nstatement at fixed $\\varepsilon$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
