    "ex-rpm-low-speed-energy-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "pthm-rpm-inverse-momentum-and-work",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, and let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future-timelike worldline in the flat Minkowski model of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. Use one inertial frame and parameterize the curve by that chart's time coordinate $t$, so its coordinates are $(ct,\\mathbf x(t))$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Identify the observer rest space with $\\mathbb R^3$ by the frame's spatial orthonormal basis and use its Euclidean dot product and norm ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). Write $\\mathbf v=d\\mathbf x/dt$, $\\gamma=(1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, proper time $\\tau$, and $U=dx/d\\tau$. In the constant-mass sector of [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]], let $m>0$ be constant and $P=mU$. In this chart write $P=(E/c,\\mathbf p)$, where $E$ and $\\mathbf p$ are the observer energy and spatial momentum of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]. Then $|\\mathbf v|<c$, $E=\\gamma mc^2>0$, $\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v$, and\n\n$$E^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2,\\qquad \\mathbf v=\\frac{c^2\\mathbf p}{E}.$$\n\nDefine the three-force by $\\mathbf F:=d\\mathbf p/dt$ and the four-force by $K:=dP/d\\tau$ in this flat inertial chart. In SI units $m$ is in kilograms, $c$ and $\\mathbf v$ in metres per second, $E$ in joules, $\\mathbf p$ in kilogram metres per second, and $\\mathbf F,K$ in newtons. Then\n\n$$\\frac{dE}{dt}=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v,\\qquad K=\\gamma\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v}{c},\\mathbf F\\right).$$\n\nSeparately, let $I_\\tau\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open proper-time interval, let $m:I_\\tau\\to(0,\\infty)$ be $C^1$, and let $U:I_\\tau\\to V$ be a $C^1$ future timelike vector field with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Measure $m$ in kilograms and $dm/d\\tau$ in kilograms per second, and set $P(\\tau):=m(\\tau)U(\\tau)$. With $K:=dP/d\\tau$, the kinematic ansatz satisfies\n\n$$\\eta(P,K)=-m\\,\\frac{dm}{d\\tau}\\,c^2.$$\n\nThe last identity is only a differentiated mass-shell relation; it prescribes no evolution for $m$. None of these conclusions assumes a force law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-rpm-low-speed-remainders",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,c>0$, let $0<\\delta<1$, and let $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$ have Euclidean norm satisfying $|v|/c\\le\\delta$. Put\n\n$$z:=\\frac{|v|^2}{c^2},\\qquad \\gamma:=(1-z)^{-1/2},\\qquad p:=m\\gamma v,\\qquad E:=\\gamma mc^2.$$\n\nThen\n\n$$0\\le\\gamma-1\\le\\frac{z}{2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad 0\\le\\gamma-1-\\frac z2\\le\\frac{3z^2}{8(1-\\delta^2)^{5/2}},$$\n\nand\n\n$$|p-mv|\\le\\frac{m|v|^3}{2c^2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad 0\\le E-mc^2-\\frac12m|v|^2\\le\\frac{3m|v|^4}{8c^2(1-\\delta^2)^{5/2}}.$$\n\nIf assigned SI kinematic units, $m$ is in kilograms, $c,v$ in metres per second, $p$ in kilogram metres per second and $E$ in joules. These are pointwise algebraic inequalities; they assume no equation of motion or empirical input.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
